130:    #  (0x3fc00003 * 0x33fffffc) + 1.0 lands just below the midpoint between
131:    #  0x3f800001 and 0x3f800002. The manual says fmac rounds ONCE -- VERIFIED
132:    #  2026-08-22 against a real copy obtained by owner directive, which this
133:    #  project did not have when the claim was written: "Rounding is performed
134:    #  once in FMAC, but twice in FADD, FSUB, and FMUL." (SH-4 Software Manual).
135:    #  The claim was made from the source and measurements alone and it HELD.
136:    #  The old
137:    #  two-rounding path collapsed onto the midpoint in double and ties-to-even then
138:    #  picked 0x3f800002. Correct under BOTH modes is 0x3f800001, so this row is
139:    #  mode-independent by value -- but the pre-#299 build fails its RN arm, which is
140:    #  what makes it a revert tripwire. Constructed and verified exactly offline.
141:    ("fmactie (a*b)+1.0", 0,
142:     {"fr0": 0x3fc00003, "fr1": 0x33fffffc, "fr2": 0x3f800000},
143:     instr(0xf21e, 2),                       # fmac fr0,fr1,fr2
144:     {RN: 0x3f800001, RZ: 0x3f800001}),
145:
146:    # ---- #299 panel: Inf must pass through the round-to-odd helper UNTOUCHED --------
147:    #  Three seats independently refuted the first helper's NaN guard (NaN != 0.0 is
148:    #  TRUE in C), under which +Inf + 1.0 came back as FLT_MAX under toward-zero and
149:    #  -Inf - 1.0 as a NaN. The offline gate watched both corruptions happen before the
150:    #  isfinite() guard existed; this row keeps the rig honest about the same contract.
151:    ("faddinf +Inf+1.0", 0,
152:     {"fr0": 0x7f800000, "fr1": 0x3f800000},
153:     instr(0xf010, 0),                       # fadd fr1,fr0
